 | 0931 | `ntdll`: a program whose name says it is a settings tool (`config`, `setting`) is a helper for the PS5 title's hand-off (`PW_HANDOFF`), as a crash reporter or an installer is: it isn't handed off, its start fails, and the game that started it carries on. Far Cry starts `Bin32\FarCryConfigurator.exe /Caller=FarCry` each time it opens and waits for it; it was handed off, so Far Cry ended and the configurator, which shows nothing after a first launch, closed at once |
 | 0932 | `win32u`: the part of the desktop a display mode smaller than it covers is centred, as win32u's `map_monitor_rect` maps the mode (scaled by the ratio of the sides that fit, about the desktop's centre); the driver took it to start at the top left. Frames show that part and the pointer stops at its edges: a 1024x768 game on a 1920x1080 desktop covers 240,0 to 1680,1080, and a game that follows the system's pointer (Sacred Underworld) reached only about five sixths of its width |
 | 0933 | `dinput`: each of the PS5 title's controllers a game finds as a DirectInput joystick (patch 0927) is also in the device manager's list, as Windows lists an Xbox 360 controller's HID device: volatile keys `HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Enum\HID\VID_045E&PID_028E&IG_0n\PS5&TITLE&n` (`Class` HIDClass, its `ClassGUID`, `DeviceDesc`, `Mfg`). A game that leaves XInput devices to XInput tells them apart by that list through WMI's `Win32_PnPEntity` (`IsXInputDevice`); Batman: Arkham Knight took the controller for another kind of joystick and never asked XInput for it |
+| 0934 | `nsi`: without `\\.\Nsi` (a PS5 title starts no `winedevice.exe`, so no `nsiproxy.sys`) the NSI requests are answered in `nsi.dll` with the software loopback interface alone, as Windows always has it: the NDIS interface table and index-to-LUID map, and the IPv4 and IPv6 interface and unicast tables (127.0.0.1/8, ::1/128), filled as `nsiproxy.sys` fills a Unix `lo`; the other tables list no rows. `GetAdaptersAddresses`, `GetIfTable`, `GetIpAddrTable` and the like failed with error 2, and Java's `NetworkInterface` threw an Error on that: Shattered Pixel Dungeon closed when it started a new game. With the device nothing changes |
 | 0972 | `faudio` (Wine's bundled FAudio 26.09): upstream FAudio a3b49d6 and 2ead980 (2026-09-26). A source voice that played at the output rate never advanced `resampleOffset`, so when it started resampling, the tap loops of `FAudio_INTERNAL_MixSource` ran from the stale offset, `toResample - tap_samples` wrapped and the SSE2 resampler wrote far past `resampled_audio`. A game corrupted its process heap and crashed in the mixer, or in the heap. FAudio is a static library, so every `xaudio2_*` and `xactengine*` module is in `PE_MODULES` |
 
